tuesday morning in steglitz and for  larissa and her children it's time for  breakfast as ukrainian refugees they're  currently living with a host family the  transition from war zone to berlin  suburb was initially hard but already  her children are learning german and  larissa says she feels this country has  been very welcoming  the government has been very supportive  they've given us money health insurance  they've helped us a lot we don't feel  like refugees we have the same rights as  germans it's helped to make us feel like  we have a second home here  in the nearly two months since the war  began many millions of people have fled  ukraine  fewer than one in ten of them has come  to germany  making the sort of journey that millions  of other refugees made in the last  decade  when the people getting off trains in  the big cities were mostly syrian  although the then german government said  it could cope with their arrival there  were many problems  once they arrived the first thing was  that they had to stay in mass  accommodation for the reception and the  reception center and that could last  months sometimes years before they found  their own housing so that was a huge  challenge and the second is when they  come with a family kids they have to go  to school so integrating them into  school learning the language  learning the normal way of the school  system although the situation for  refugees here is better now that  experience has etched itself onto the  collective memory and forced politicians  to change policy  the new government has offered support  on a large scale to the federal states  this has allowed us to give refugees  better opportunities for integration  this is a clear message we're doing it  better we're doing it differently and  we've learned from the mistakes of the  past  there is another important difference  between then and now  ukrainians have the right to visa-free  travel across much of the eu  syrians do not  which helps to explain why most of the  ukrainian refugees in europe have not  come to germany whereas most of the  syrians did so when ministers say that  they are dealing with the problems now  better  that might in part be because the need  for resources is being tested far less  than it was back then  dominic kane al jazeera berlin
 

